\date{2026-09-06}
\title{Flatness of the interval}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{The intended model of triangulated type theory is the topos of cubical
spaces, presheaves on the Dedekind cube category, which classifies
[flat distributive lattices](ub-004Q); the interval is the generic one. This
module considers flatness of #{\II}, in two forms (\ref{ub-0040}): as a
property of #{\II} among all distributive lattices, and in a crisp form,
which is how the external statement reads in the internal language. It then
compares flatness with the other axioms on the interval.}

\p{The two forms agree once #{0 \neq 1} and the crisp points of #{\II} are
#{0} and #{1} (\ref{ub-0050}). From flatness, #{\II} is nontrivial, local and
colocal (\ref{ub-004S}), for the reasons any flat lattice is; this needs
neither cohesion nor any other axiom. In the other direction, crisp flatness
follows from SQCP together with the [density of crisp cubes](ub-004F), and
so from SQCP and the [separation of cubes](stt-00J7) (\ref{ub-004C}). The
connection is that a
crisp cube in the spectrum of an algebra is, by the generalised Phoa
principle, a crisp homomorphism into polynomials (\ref{ub-004L}). None of
this is a characterisation, and \ref{ub-0041} explains why none in finitely
many coherent sequents can be.}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice

module Synthetic.Flatness
  {@♭ 𝓘} (@♭ Δ¹ : Type 𝓘) (@♭ I : Lattice Δ¹)
  (@♭ I-distr : is-distributive I)
  where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Fin

open import Modalities.Cohesion.Flat
open import Modalities.Cohesion.FlatDiscrete
open import Modalities.Cohesion.FlatConnectedMaps
open import Modalities.Cohesion.Continuity
open import Modalities.Instances.Truncation

open import Algebra.Lattice.Flat
open import Algebra.Lattice.Crisp
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Bool
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.BaseChange
  Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Multivariate I I-distr
open import Algebra.Lattice.Presentations I I-distr

open import Synthetic.Quasicoherence Δ¹ I I-distr
open import Synthetic.Boundaries Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.DetectingEquivalencesCovariantFamilies Δ¹ I
  using (module ♭Δ-Induction)
open import Synthetic.Cubes Δ¹ i0 i1
open import Synthetic.CubeDensity Δ¹ i0 i1
open import Synthetic.Duality Δ¹ I I-distr
  using (cube-hom; cube-hom-at)

open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SetQuotients

open import Foundations.InjectiveMaps

import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Multivariate as Multivariate
import Algebra.Lattice.Presentations as Presentations
}
}

\subtree[ub-0040]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Flatness of the interval}

\p{The interval is \em{flat} when it is a [flat distributive
lattice](ub-004Q).}

\agda{
interval-is-flat : Type 𝓘
interval-is-flat = is-flat I I-distr
}

\p{Externally, flatness of the generic lattice is a scheme of geometric
sequents with Boolean parameters. Read in triangulated type theory those
external data become crisp: a crisp presentation over #{\II} has Boolean
parameters, since #{\modflat{\II} \simeq \mathrm{Bool}}, and the factorisation
#{h : A \to \II[y_1, \ldots, y_n]} is a crisp homomorphism. The point #{y}
of the cube at which it is [evaluated](ub-004M) is not crisp, and neither is
the point #{x} of the spectrum being factored. So \em{crisp flatness} says:
every point of the spectrum of a crisp finitely presented algebra lies on a
crisp cube in it, where a crisp cube is a crisp homomorphism to polynomials
rather than a crisp map from #{\II^n} (but see \ref{ub-004L}). Under mild
assumptions on the crisp points of #{\II}, crisp flatness implies flatness
(\ref{ub-0050}).}

\agda{
module _ (@♭ p : Presentation) where

  factors-through-crisp-cube : ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ → Spec (Presented p) → Type 𝓘
  factors-through-crisp-cube (mod♭ n) x
    = Σ[ h ∶ ⟨♭| LatticeAlgHom (Presented p) (Polynomial-Algebra n) ⟩ ]
      Σ[ y ∶ (Fin n → Δ¹) ]
        (x ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval-at n y) (ε♭ h))

interval-is-crisply-flat : Type 𝓘
interval-is-crisply-flat
  = (@♭ p : Presentation) (x : Spec (Presented p))
  → ║ Σ[ n ∶ ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ ] factors-through-crisp-cube p n x ║₋₁
}
}

\subtree[ub-0050]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Crisp flatness implies flatness}
\date{2026-10-07}

\p{Suppose #{0 \neq 1} and the boundary inclusion #{\partial\Delta^1 \to \II}
is #{\flat}-surjective, both crisply. Then the crisp points of #{\II} are
exactly the images of #{\mathrm{Bool}}, and crisp flatness implies
flatness.}

\p{The map #{\mathrm{Bool} \to \II} is injective because #{0 \neq 1}, and a
crisp point of #{\II} is #{0} or #{1} by \ref{stt-00UT}.}

\agda{
module _
  (@♭ 0≠1 : i0 ≠ i1)
  (@♭ ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective : is-♭-surjective ∂Δ¹-incl)
  where
  open ♭Δ-Induction ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective

  Bool-ι-is-injective : is-injective (Algebra.ι (Bool-Algebra I))
  Bool-ι-is-injective {true} {true} e = refl
  Bool-ι-is-injective {true} {false} e = ¡ (0≠1 (sym e))
  Bool-ι-is-injective {false} {true} e = ¡ (0≠1 e)
  Bool-ι-is-injective {false} {false} e = refl

  Bool-ι-lift : (@♭ i : Δ¹) → fibre (Algebra.ι (Bool-Algebra I)) i
  Bool-ι-lift
    = ♭Δ¹-ind
        (λ i → fibre (Algebra.ι (Bool-Algebra I)) i
             , is-prop-map←is-embedding
                 (is-embedding←is-injective carrier-is-set Bool-ι-is-injective)
                 i)
        (false , refl) (true , refl)
}

\p{Now let #{x : A \to \II} be a homomorphism out of a lattice presented over
#{\mathrm{Bool}}. Presentations over #{\mathrm{Bool}} are #{\flat}-discrete
(\ref{ub-004Y}), so we may assume the presentation is crisp. Its
[base change](ub-0051) is a crisp presentation over #{\II}, of an algebra
#{A_\II} whose points are those of #{A}, and crisp flatness factors the
point #{x} of #{\Spec(A_\II)} as a crisp #{h_\II : A_\II \to
\II[y_1, \ldots, y_n]} followed by evaluation at some #{y}. The images of the
generators under #{h_\II} are crisp polynomials over #{\II}, so they are base
changes of polynomials #{q_k} over #{\mathrm{Bool}} (\ref{ub-004Z}). These
satisfy the relations, base change being injective (\ref{ub-004X}), so they
define #{h : A \to \mathrm{Bool}[y_1, \ldots, y_n]}, and #{x} is #{h}
followed by evaluation at #{y}, as both agree on the generators.}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{\mathrm{Bool}[x_1, \ldots, x_k]} && A
	&& {\mathrm{Bool}[y_1, \ldots, y_n]} && \II \\
	\\
	{\II[x_1, \ldots, x_k]} && {A_\II} && {\II[y_1, \ldots, y_n]}
	\arrow[two heads, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["h", dashed, from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow["{\mathrm{ev}_y}", from=1-5, to=1-7]
	\arrow["x", curve={height=-24pt}, from=1-3, to=1-7]
	\arrow[two heads, from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{h_\II}"', from=3-3, to=3-5]
	\arrow["{\mathrm{ev}_y}"', from=3-5, to=1-7]
	\arrow["\beta"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\beta", hook, from=1-5, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{Here #{\beta} is base change, the left square is the
[unit](ub-0051) #{A \to A_\II} of base change of the presentation, and the
triangle on the right is \ref{ub-004W}. The
crisp map #{h_\II} and the point #{y} come from crisp flatness, applied to
the map #{A_\II \to \II} induced by #{x}. The dashed #{h} is what is
constructed: on generators it is the lift of #{h_\II} along #{\beta}, so the
middle square commutes, and then so does the whole diagram.}

\agda{
  interval-is-flat←crisply-flat
    : interval-is-crisply-flat → interval-is-flat
  interval-is-flat←crisply-flat cflat
    = ♭-discrete-ind
        (Presentation-is-♭-discrete Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive
          Bool-is-♭-discrete)
        _
        crisp-case
    where
      module BMv = Multivariate Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive
      module BP = Presentations Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive

      I' : Algebra Bool-Lattice Δ¹
      I' = Algebra-res (Bool-Algebra I) (initial-Algebra I)

      E : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → Δ¹) → BMv.Polynomials n → Δ¹
      E n w = BMv.Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr n w .fst .fst

      base-change-eval
        : (n : ℕ) (w : Fin n → Δ¹) (q : BMv.Polynomials n)
        → Polynomial-eval-at n w .fst .fst (base-change n q) ＝ E n w q
      base-change-eval n w q
        = Polynomial-eval-base-change (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n w q
        ∙ happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) I'-eval) q
        where
          I'-eval
            : BMv.Polynomial-eval I' I-distr n w
            ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘
                (Algebra-id-hom ~refl (initial-lattice-algebra I))
                (BMv.Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr n w)
          I'-eval
            = unap-equiv (BMv.Polynomial-ev-is-equiv I' I-distr n)
                (funext→ λ k →
                  BMv.Polynomial-eval-gen I' I-distr n w k
                  ∙ sym (BMv.Polynomial-eval-gen (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr n w k))

      crisp-case
        : (@♭ p : Presentations.Presentation Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive)
          (x : LatticeAlgHom (BP.Presented p) (Bool-Algebra I))
        → ║ factors-through-free I I-distr p x ║₋₁
      crisp-case p@(BP.mk-presentation g r lhs rhs) x
        = Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
            (λ where (mod♭ n , mod♭ hI , y , e) → from-cube n hI y e)
            (cflat (base-change-presentation p) xI)
        where
          pI = base-change-presentation p

          xI : Spec (Presented pI)
          xI = Presented-base-change-extend p (initial-Algebra I) I-distr
                 (LatticeAlgHom-∘
                   (Algebra-id-hom ~refl (initial-lattice-algebra I)) x)

          from-cube
            : (@♭ n : ℕ)
              (@♭ hI : LatticeAlgHom (Presented (base-change-presentation p))
                         (Polynomial-Algebra n))
              (y : Fin n → Δ¹)
            → xI ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval-at n y) hI
            → ║ factors-through-free I I-distr p x ║₋₁
          from-cube n hI y e = Trunc.η (n , h , y , x＝)
            where
              lift
                : (k : Fin g)
                → fibre (base-change n)
                    (hI .fst .fst (ι-quot (Polynomial-gen k)))
              lift
                = base-change-lift-family Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive
                    (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr Bool-ι-is-injective Bool-ι-lift
                    (Fin-is-♭-discrete g) n
                    (λ k → hI .fst .fst (ι-quot (Polynomial-gen k)))

              q : Fin g → BMv.Polynomials n
              q k = lift k .fst

              G : LatticeAlgHom (BMv.Polynomial-Algebra g)
                    (BMv.Polynomial-Algebra n)
              G = BMv.Polynomial-eval (BMv.Polynomial-Algebra n)
                    (BMv.Polynomial-distr n) g q

              G-gen : (k : Fin g) → G .fst .fst (BMv.Polynomial-gen k) ＝ q k
              G-gen = BMv.Polynomial-eval-gen (BMv.Polynomial-Algebra n)
                        (BMv.Polynomial-distr n) g q

              base-change-G
                : (t : BMv.Polynomials g)
                → base-change n (G .fst .fst t)
                ＝ hI .fst .fst (ι-quot (base-change g t))
              base-change-G t
                = happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) on-gens) t
                where
                  on-gens
                    : LatticeAlgHom-∘ (base-change-hom n) G
                    ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘
                        (Algebra-res-hom (Bool-Algebra I)
                          (LatticeAlgHom-∘ hI (Presented-ι pI)))
                        (base-change-hom g)
                  on-gens
                    = unap-equiv
                        (BMv.Polynomial-ev-is-equiv
                          (Algebra-res (Bool-Algebra I) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
                          (Polynomial-distr n) g)
                        (funext→ λ k →
                          ap (base-change n) (G-gen k)
                          ∙ lift k .snd
                          ∙ sym (ap (λ t → hI .fst .fst (ι-quot t))
                                  (base-change-gen k)))

              G-respects : BP.respects p G
              G-respects k
                = base-change-is-injective Bool-ι-is-injective n
                    ( base-change-G (lhs k)
                    ∙ ap (hI .fst .fst) (relation-holds pI k)
                    ∙ sym (base-change-G (rhs k)))

              h : LatticeAlgHom (BP.Presented p) (BMv.Polynomial-Algebra n)
              h = BP.Presented-rec p G G-respects

              x＝ : x ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘
                          (BMv.Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra I) I-distr n y) h
              x＝
                = BP.Presented-hom-ext p I-distr _ _ λ k →
                    sym (Presented-base-change-extend-gen p (initial-Algebra I)
                          I-distr _ k)
                  ∙ happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) e)
                      (ι-quot (Polynomial-gen k))
                  ∙ ap (Polynomial-eval-at n y .fst .fst) (sym (lift k .snd))
                  ∙ base-change-eval n y (q k)
                  ∙ ap (E n y) (sym (G-gen k))
                  ∙ ap (E n y)
                      (sym (BP.Presented-rec-β p G G-respects
                             (BMv.Polynomial-gen k)))
}
}

\subtree[ub-004S]{
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{A flat interval is nontrivial, local and colocal}
\date{2026-10-07}

\p{If the interval is flat, then #{0 \neq 1}, #{i \lor j = 1} implies
#{i = 1} or #{j = 1}, and #{i \land j = 0} implies #{i = 0} or #{j = 0}.
These are \ref{ub-004R}, \ref{ub-004B} and \ref{ub-004E} for #{\II}.}

\p{The disjunctive property obtained this way replaces the directedness and
the separation by types with initial objects that \ref{stt-0114} uses, and
needs neither duality nor SQCP.}

\agda{
interval-is-nontrivial←flat : interval-is-flat → i0 ≠ i1
interval-is-nontrivial←flat = flat-is-nontrivial I I-distr

disjunctive-property←flat
  : interval-is-flat → (i j : Δ¹) → i ∨ j ＝ i1 → (i ＝ i1) * (j ＝ i1)
disjunctive-property←flat = flat-is-local I I-distr

conjunctive-property←flat
  : interval-is-flat → (i j : Δ¹) → i ∧ j ＝ i0 → (i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0)
conjunctive-property←flat = flat-is-colocal I I-distr
}
}

\subtree[ub-004C]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Density of cubes implies flatness}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{If [SQCP](stt-00TC) holds and the crisp cubes are [dense](ub-004F) in sets,
then the interval is [crisply flat](ub-0040). Fix a crisp presentation with
algebra #{A} and a point #{x} of #{\Spec(A)}, which is a set. By density #{x}
merely lies on a crisp cube #{g : \II^m \to \Spec(A)}, say #{x = g(z)}. By
\ref{ub-004L}, #{g} is evaluation of a crisp map of algebras #{h : A \to
\II[y_1, \ldots, y_m]}, and so #{x} is #{h} evaluated at #{z}.}

\agda{
interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-are-dense
  : @♭ SQCP → cubes-are-dense-for-sets → interval-is-crisply-flat
interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-are-dense sqcp dense p x
  = Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
      (λ where
        (mod♭ (m , g) , c , e) →
          Trunc.η
            ( mod♭ m
            , mod♭ (cube-hom sqcp (Presented p) m (g ∘ □←cube))
            , cube←□ c
            , sym e
            ∙ ap g (sym (□←cube-retr c))
            ∙ cube-hom-at sqcp (Presented p) m (g ∘ □←cube) (cube←□ c)))
      (dense (Spec (Presented p)) (Spec-is-set (Presented p)) x)
}

\p{Density for sets is [separation](stt-00J7) for maps between sets
(\ref{ub-004H}), so crisp flatness follows from the axiom that the cubes
separate.
In the intended model this is the observation that every element of a
presheaf lies in the image of a representable. Separation quantifies over all
crisp types, so this is no finite axiom either, in keeping with
\ref{ub-0041}.}

\agda{
interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-separate
  : @♭ SQCP → family-separates □^_ → interval-is-crisply-flat
interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-separate sqcp sep
  = interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-are-dense sqcp
      (cubes-are-dense-for-sets←cubes-separate-sets
        (cubes-separate-sets←cubes-separate sep))
}

\p{With #{0 \neq 1} and the #{\flat}-surjectivity of the boundary, both of
which hold in triangulated type theory, crisp flatness is flatness
(\ref{ub-0050}).}

\agda{
module _
  (@♭ 0≠1 : i0 ≠ i1)
  (@♭ ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective : is-♭-surjective ∂Δ¹-incl)
  (@♭ sqcp : SQCP)
  where

  interval-is-flat←cubes-are-dense
    : cubes-are-dense-for-sets → interval-is-flat
  interval-is-flat←cubes-are-dense dense
    = interval-is-flat←crisply-flat 0≠1 ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective
        (interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-are-dense sqcp dense)

  interval-is-flat←cubes-separate
    : family-separates □^_ → interval-is-flat
  interval-is-flat←cubes-separate sep
    = interval-is-flat←crisply-flat 0≠1 ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective
        (interval-is-crisply-flat←cubes-separate sqcp sep)
}
}
